WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.067 --> 00:00:02.267
-Well, hey, how about this one?

00:00:02.267 --> 00:00:03.501
Regarding my panic attacks,

00:00:03.501 --> 00:00:05.734
I've had more psychotic episodes
than "Twin Peaks."

00:00:05.734 --> 00:00:08.734
[ Laughter ]

00:00:08.734 --> 00:00:12.300
I mean, I'm so crazy...
-How crazy are you?

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:13.434
-There we go.

00:00:13.434 --> 00:00:15.400
[ Laughter ]

00:00:17.734 --> 00:00:19.133
-Welcome back to "Late Night."

00:00:19.133 --> 00:00:20.701
We're here with Jason Sudeikis.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:20.701 --> 00:00:24.734
That was a clip from the third
season of "Ted Lasso."

00:00:24.734 --> 00:00:26.367
We talked about this
in the past.

00:00:26.367 --> 00:00:29.234
You've been on the show
with Joe Kelly, Brendan Hunt.

00:00:29.234 --> 00:00:31.400
I've known all you guys
for over two decades.

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:33.634
We worked together in Amsterdam.

00:00:33.634 --> 00:00:36.100
One of the things we spent time
doing was playing FIFA.

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:38.534
-Yeah.
-And, I mean, I never would have

00:00:38.534 --> 00:00:40.767
predicted this.
You are now a character in FIFA.

00:00:40.767 --> 00:00:45.167
-Yeah.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:45.167 --> 00:00:47.567
I mean, yeah.

00:00:47.567 --> 00:00:49.267
It's --

00:00:49.267 --> 00:00:52.033
Again, and this is nice.
Otis will play FIFA.

00:00:52.033 --> 00:00:53.501
This was a big deal
to everybody in the cast.

00:00:53.501 --> 00:00:56.400
But we -- whenever Otis sees
that, he goes, "Why --

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:58.133
It looks like you,
but you're so mad."

00:00:58.133 --> 00:00:59.200
[ Laughter ]

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:00.868
And as a dad, I was like,

00:01:00.868 --> 00:01:03.567
"He doesn't view me that way,"
which I thought was so nice.

00:01:03.567 --> 00:01:04.734
I was like --
You know what I mean?

00:01:04.734 --> 00:01:06.100
'Cause you're always like,
"Don't do that!

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:07.801
Put that down!"
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:01:07.801 --> 00:01:09.868
-No, he's like, "No, this is
a guy who's very upset

00:01:09.868 --> 00:01:12.467
at his defensive team."
His defense --

00:01:12.467 --> 00:01:14.868
And it's very different
from the "Ted Lasso" character.

00:01:14.868 --> 00:01:16.133
But I think, you know,
they just --

00:01:16.133 --> 00:01:18.300
-Whereas, when my son
sees this show, he's like,

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:20.300
"Why are you so happy?"
[ Laughter ]

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:22.334
-"Why are you talking so much?"
[ Laughter ]

00:01:22.334 --> 00:01:24.400
"Why are you so interested
in these other people's --

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:25.801
these stranger's jobs?"

00:01:25.801 --> 00:01:27.200
-"Yeah, why not
just let them be?"

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:28.434
-"You don't watch
any of this stuff.

00:01:28.434 --> 00:01:30.200
You say you like all of it.

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:32.501
You sit in your office
and do nothing."

00:01:32.501 --> 00:01:36.100
-"Is there ever gonna be a
'Closer Look' about me, Dad?"

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:39.300
-"Take a closer look here,
papa!"

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:41.701
[ Laughter ]

00:01:41.701 --> 00:01:44.334
They don't get it.
It's like, "Oh, yeah?

00:01:44.334 --> 00:01:46.567
You go buy your own food,
you little son of a --"

00:01:46.567 --> 00:01:48.400
[ Laughter ]

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:52.767
-Talk to my wife.
If I have a catchphrase at home,

00:01:52.767 --> 00:01:55.000
it's, "You go buy your own food,
you son of a --"

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:57.167
[ Laughter ]

00:01:57.167 --> 00:01:59.701
-The Great Santini.
Lines that got cut.

00:01:59.701 --> 00:02:01.968
-[ Laughs ]
-But, yeah, this thing meant

00:02:01.968 --> 00:02:04.367
more to any of the fellas
that we were looking at

00:02:04.367 --> 00:02:06.033
on this show
that play on the cast.

00:02:06.033 --> 00:02:07.300
They get to be on that game.

00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:08.801
They've been playing it
for as long as we have.

00:02:08.801 --> 00:02:10.300
But since they were little kids,
even.

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:13.033
And, like, two Emmy's --
la-de-da.

00:02:13.033 --> 00:02:15.434
To be an actual player on there,
yeah, was, like,

00:02:15.434 --> 00:02:18.100
I think it helped relationships
with their fathers, you know?

00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:19.501
[ Laughter ]
That were like,

00:02:19.501 --> 00:02:21.133
"I'm going to be an artist,
Dad."

00:02:21.133 --> 00:02:22.767
And they were like, "You go
buy your own food, you --"

00:02:22.767 --> 00:02:23.901
[ Laughter ]

00:02:23.901 --> 00:02:25.767
It's like,
"Dad, I'm a footballer.

00:02:25.767 --> 00:02:27.734
I'm a real fake footballer."

00:02:27.734 --> 00:02:29.601
[ Laughter ]

00:02:29.601 --> 00:02:31.300
-Now, have you noticed this?
Because I will say,

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:33.334
a couple years ago,
much like any athlete

00:02:33.334 --> 00:02:35.167
who hangs it up,
because I played FIFA

00:02:35.167 --> 00:02:36.567
for like 20 straight years.

00:02:36.567 --> 00:02:38.033
I realized
I just don't have it anymore.

00:02:38.033 --> 00:02:39.834
-Yeah.
-You know, at some point,

00:02:39.834 --> 00:02:41.400
you just lose the touch.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:45.067
No, mine's gotten better,
weirdly, because I understand

00:02:45.067 --> 00:02:47.033
the game a tad bit better
from just hanging out

00:02:47.033 --> 00:02:48.501
with Brendan
and going to see matches.

00:02:48.501 --> 00:02:49.634
-Yeah.
-We try to go, like,

00:02:49.634 --> 00:02:51.067
every week to something.

00:02:51.067 --> 00:02:52.367
And this last season was great.

00:02:52.367 --> 00:02:53.634
Season, two we didn't get
to do much because everybody

00:02:53.634 --> 00:02:55.901
was still on lockdown.

00:02:55.901 --> 00:02:58.100
But by this point in the show,
it was so well-received

00:02:58.100 --> 00:03:00.300
that people were like,
"Come watch, come watch."

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:02.200
And so we'd go.
And then I'm kind of like,

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:04.100
"Okay, I think I understand
what's going on here."

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:06.868
And then, when playing with
our own players, I was like,

00:03:06.868 --> 00:03:08.434
"Well, I know
what their skills are."

00:03:08.434 --> 00:03:10.701
You know,
"This guy hits his mark.

00:03:10.701 --> 00:03:12.767
He shows up late."
[ Laughter ]

00:03:12.767 --> 00:03:14.868
You know, "He improvises well."

00:03:14.868 --> 00:03:17.868
But, it's -- I've gotten
a little bit better.

00:03:17.868 --> 00:03:19.200
You know, oddly.
-Oh, good.

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:20.367
-And I think, you know,

00:03:20.367 --> 00:03:21.634
are you playing games
with your kids yet?

00:03:21.634 --> 00:03:23.234
-Not yet.
-Yeah.

00:03:23.234 --> 00:03:25.934
That's -- That's where, I think,
it's gonna get tricky

00:03:25.934 --> 00:03:28.400
because I know he will
soon overpass me.

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:29.968
-Right.
That's gonna be tough.

00:03:29.968 --> 00:03:31.567
-Yeah, yeah, that's gonna be --
that's not gonna be

00:03:31.567 --> 00:03:34.267
on a driveway, though,
where I get hurt.

00:03:34.267 --> 00:03:36.234
It's just gonna be my ego.

00:03:36.234 --> 00:03:37.801
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:37.801 --> 00:03:39.334
-"You go buy
your own controllers."

00:03:39.334 --> 00:03:40.868
[ Laughter ]

00:03:40.868 --> 00:03:44.501
-Do you -- have the kids
discovered any of your

00:03:44.501 --> 00:03:45.968
"SNL" work from the past?
-Yeah.

00:03:45.968 --> 00:03:47.734
-They have?
-Literally in the past couple --

00:03:47.734 --> 00:03:49.601
in the past month.

00:03:49.601 --> 00:03:51.000
And they've asked.
They've asked.

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:53.100
I remember showing them some --
or I think they watched

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:58.000
when I hosted October --
two Octobers ago.

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:01.501
And then, when you get into,
you know, the YouTube algorithm,

00:04:01.501 --> 00:04:03.334
then they just start showing up
on the side,

00:04:03.334 --> 00:04:04.367
and they want to watch them.

00:04:04.367 --> 00:04:06.968
And we watched
some of the best ones --

00:04:06.968 --> 00:04:09.067
I did this sketch.
You'll know.

00:04:09.067 --> 00:04:11.033
You probably won't.
"Maine Justice."

00:04:11.033 --> 00:04:13.133
-"Maine Justice," classic.
[ Cheers ]

00:04:13.133 --> 00:04:15.000
I think we have a photo
of "Maine Justice."

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:17.267
Maine Justice, here you are
with Jamie Foxx.

00:04:17.267 --> 00:04:19.434
Did they follow "Maine Justice"?
-No. No one does.

00:04:19.434 --> 00:04:21.467
-No one does,
I was gonna say.

00:04:21.467 --> 00:04:27.067
-You and Lorne were very picky
about wanting context and logic.

00:04:27.067 --> 00:04:30.734
-I would like to concede
the point now that I was wrong.

00:04:30.734 --> 00:04:33.100
You know?
-That's very, very big of you.

00:04:33.100 --> 00:04:34.834
-I had a lot of questions
about the logic

00:04:34.834 --> 00:04:36.400
behind "Maine Justice,"
and then you did it

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:38.067
for the audience, and I'm like,
"They don't care."

00:04:38.067 --> 00:04:40.133
-They don't care.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:40.133 --> 00:04:41.801
And yet I'm logic cop
on our show.

00:04:41.801 --> 00:04:44.367
So I've taken it elsewhere
and driven people crazy.

00:04:44.367 --> 00:04:46.767
-I feel bad -- I do feel bad
because after this crushed

00:04:46.767 --> 00:04:47.934
during that commercial break,

00:04:47.934 --> 00:04:49.234
I went out
and I yelled at the audience.

00:04:49.234 --> 00:04:51.067
I'm like,
"I tried to stand up for you!"

00:04:51.067 --> 00:04:52.400
[ Laughter ]

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:54.467
"And it turns out you didn't
care about any of it!"

00:04:54.467 --> 00:04:57.300
-I remember in my office, like,
O'Brien -- and Mike O'Brien

00:04:57.300 --> 00:05:00.934
was my office mate and defending
that when Lorne would be like,

00:05:00.934 --> 00:05:02.567
"What's the logic here?"
and it's like,

00:05:02.567 --> 00:05:05.734
"Man, you gave us
Toonces the Driving Cat, dude.

00:05:05.734 --> 00:05:07.467
You forged this."
-Yeah.

00:05:07.467 --> 00:05:09.968
"You made this idiocy.

00:05:09.968 --> 00:05:12.334
Like, what was the logic there,
man?"

00:05:12.334 --> 00:05:13.801
No.
-"Maine Justice."

00:05:13.801 --> 00:05:15.167
-"Maine Justice,"
and then Potato Chip

00:05:15.167 --> 00:05:16.534
is literally the same character

00:05:16.534 --> 00:05:17.834
[Cajun accent]
where I sorta talk like this.

00:05:17.834 --> 00:05:19.234
[ Laughter ]

00:05:19.234 --> 00:05:22.067
[Normal voice] And so,
Otis was doing the voice, like,

00:05:22.067 --> 00:05:23.400
a few days later.

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:26.300
I'm just doing George Kennedy
from "Cool Hand Luke."

00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:28.634
I'm doing like Foghorn Leghorn.

00:05:28.634 --> 00:05:31.801
And when you hear your little
8 1/2-year-Old son do it,

00:05:31.801 --> 00:05:34.267
it sounds problematic.
-Yeah.

00:05:34.267 --> 00:05:35.968
[ Laughter ]
-Because I'm like, well --

00:05:35.968 --> 00:05:38.067
I'm like, "What are you doing?
What are you --"

00:05:38.067 --> 00:05:40.267
And then I realize,
"Oh, he's doing Maine Justice."

00:05:40.267 --> 00:05:42.133
He's doing that.
-That's a tricky thing

00:05:42.133 --> 00:05:44.667
when the principal calls
and says, "Your son,

00:05:44.667 --> 00:05:46.868
he's telling us he's doing
'Maine Justice.'"

00:05:46.868 --> 00:05:48.734
-Yeah, exactly!
-"And our question for you is,

00:05:48.734 --> 00:05:51.167
what's the logic?"
-Exactly!

00:05:51.167 --> 00:05:53.601
[ Laughter ]

00:05:53.601 --> 00:05:55.067
You know, and that's an L.A.
school for you.

00:05:55.067 --> 00:05:56.234
Everybody's in the business.

00:05:56.234 --> 00:05:58.901
A lot of studio execs.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:58.901 --> 00:06:01.000
That's their fallback career
is principals.

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:02.367
But I --
[ Laughter ]

00:06:02.367 --> 00:06:07.067
But then they've -- "A-Holes,"
Kristen Wiig and I did.

00:06:07.067 --> 00:06:09.467
-Gotcha.
I mean, 9 and 6.

00:06:09.467 --> 00:06:11.100
Do they know
what A-hole stands for?

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:12.434
-Yeah.
Yeah, yeah.

00:06:12.434 --> 00:06:13.667
[ Laughter ]

00:06:13.667 --> 00:06:14.934
I told them apathy.

00:06:14.934 --> 00:06:16.234
[ Laughter ]

00:06:16.234 --> 00:06:17.934
-Apathy's pretty on the money
for the A-holes.

00:06:17.934 --> 00:06:19.801
-Yeah, and they're like,
"Oh, you mean the anus?"

00:06:19.801 --> 00:06:21.067
I go, "How did you
put that together?"

00:06:21.067 --> 00:06:24.100
No, they didn't really.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:24.100 --> 00:06:28.300
And then, last week,

00:06:28.300 --> 00:06:30.968
I showed them Roundball Rock,
which was a sketch

00:06:30.968 --> 00:06:33.367
that Tim Robinson
and I believe Zach Cannon wrote.

00:06:33.367 --> 00:06:36.534
-Uh-huh.
-Where we played John Tesh --

00:06:36.534 --> 00:06:37.734
-And Dave Tesh.

00:06:37.734 --> 00:06:39.267
-And he plays his brother,
Dave Tesh.

00:06:39.267 --> 00:06:40.434
-Roundball Rock.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:40.434 --> 00:06:42.567
This is great.
And this is great.

00:06:42.567 --> 00:06:45.133
Tim now "I Think
You Should Leave" fame.

00:06:45.133 --> 00:06:47.200
-Yeah, yeah.
Emmy Award winner.

00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:48.701
-Emmy Award winner.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:06:48.701 --> 00:06:50.801
-And fantastic dude, all around.

00:06:50.801 --> 00:06:51.801
And this is a great one.

00:06:51.801 --> 00:06:53.234
So, your kids like that one,
as well?

00:06:53.234 --> 00:06:55.467
-Yes. And they sing the song --
Ba-ba-ba-ba-ba-basketball.

00:06:55.467 --> 00:06:57.133
It's like, there's just lyrics.

00:06:57.133 --> 00:06:59.634
Dave Tesh wrote lyrics to
John Tesh's instrumental,

00:06:59.634 --> 00:07:01.634
like, famous --
♪ Dun dun dun dun dun, da da ♪

00:07:01.634 --> 00:07:04.634
and it's just him going
♪ Ba-ba-ba-ba-basketball ♪

00:07:04.634 --> 00:07:07.033
♪ Gimme gimme gimme the ball
'cause I'm gonna dunk it ♪

00:07:07.033 --> 00:07:09.100
Yeah, and just repeats it
over and over.

00:07:09.100 --> 00:07:11.601
So I just hear Otis and Daisy
getting ready in the morning.

00:07:11.601 --> 00:07:13.167
♪ Ba-ba-ba-ba-ba-basketball ♪

00:07:13.167 --> 00:07:14.167
[ Laughter ]

00:07:14.167 --> 00:07:15.434
I was like, "We're doing it.

00:07:15.434 --> 00:07:16.734
We're doing God's work."

00:07:16.734 --> 00:07:19.567
-And is it true from A-holes
that they only do you?

00:07:19.567 --> 00:07:21.434
-They only do me, yeah.
Like, yeah,

00:07:21.434 --> 00:07:23.634
'cause it's the easier part
to play. Right?

00:07:23.634 --> 00:07:25.033
Kristen's bringing nuance to it.

00:07:25.033 --> 00:07:27.501
I'm just, like, a belligerent,
like, gum-chomping A-hole.

00:07:27.501 --> 00:07:28.667
Right?
But, yeah.

00:07:28.667 --> 00:07:30.267
They're just like,
"Hey babe, hey, babe."

00:07:30.267 --> 00:07:31.601
Just doing the whole thing.

00:07:31.601 --> 00:07:33.267
And it's like, love it.

00:07:33.267 --> 00:07:37.601
-We -- I met my wife Alexi,
you know, during --

00:07:37.601 --> 00:07:39.734
it was during the A-hole years
of "SNL."

00:07:39.734 --> 00:07:42.667
And we just started
ironically calling each other

00:07:42.667 --> 00:07:44.934
"babe" like that sketch.

00:07:44.934 --> 00:07:46.734
Terrible mistake 'cause it
[bleep] stuck.

00:07:46.734 --> 00:07:48.067
-Mm-hmm.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:48.067 --> 00:07:49.701
Yeah.
-And now, like, here it is,

00:07:49.701 --> 00:07:51.934
whatever, 15 years later,
and I'm walking around

00:07:51.934 --> 00:07:54.234
in front of my kids, like,
without irony now, being like,

00:07:54.234 --> 00:07:55.567
"Hey, babe, hey, babe."
[ Laughter ]

00:07:55.567 --> 00:07:58.000
I'm like, "How did this happen?"
Be careful.

00:07:58.000 --> 00:07:59.601
-I know.
We tapped into something there.

00:07:59.601 --> 00:08:01.033
As long as you're saying
please and thank you,

00:08:01.033 --> 00:08:03.200
no one's gonna call you A-holes.

00:08:03.200 --> 00:08:06.300
That was just two people
being really mean to people

00:08:06.300 --> 00:08:08.934
that were trying to help.
-Yep, that was the core of it.

00:08:08.934 --> 00:08:11.234
-And they loved each other.
-They did love each other.

00:08:11.234 --> 00:08:13.000
-He loved her.
He loved --

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:14.968
Jason's character
loved Kristen's character.

00:08:14.968 --> 00:08:16.667
But A-holes
to the rest of the world.

00:08:16.667 --> 00:08:19.167
-There you go.
Hey, man, congratulations

00:08:19.167 --> 00:08:20.267
on another great season.
-Thank you.

00:08:20.267 --> 00:08:21.400
-It's so lovely
to have you back here.

00:08:21.400 --> 00:08:22.534
-Great to be back.
-Really appreciate it.

00:08:22.534 --> 00:08:24.300
[ Cheers and applause ]
Jason Sudeikis.

00:08:24.300 --> 00:08:25.634
Third season, "Ted Lasso,"

00:08:25.634 --> 00:08:27.968
streaming on apple TV+.
New episodes on Wednesdays.

00:08:27.968 --> 00:08:29.467
We'll be right back with
Annaleigh Ashford.

